\section{Problem Setup and Methods}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Safety Commitment Point}
\label{sec:commitment-point}

When a reasoning model is jailbroken, its chain-of-thought (CoT) transitions from safety-aware deliberation to committed harmful generation. We define the \textbf{Safety Commitment Point} (CP) as the first reasoning step after which safety reasoning permanently ceases. Steps before CP are \emph{pre-commitment} ($y_t = 0$); steps at or after CP are \emph{post-commitment} ($y_t = 1$). The detection task produces a score $s_t \in [0, 1]$ estimating whether the model has passed its CP by step $t$.

We study four reasoning models spanning two architectures and two scales (\cref{tab:models}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Arch.} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{Layers} & \textbf{Hidden} & \textbf{$n$ (total\,/\,test)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & Llama & 8B & 32 & 4096 & 209\,/\,43 \\
OT-7B & Qwen & 7B & 28 & 3584 & 228\,/\,47 \\
R1-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 100\,/\,20 \\
QwQ-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 95\,/\,19 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Models studied. R1-8B and R1-32B are distilled from DeepSeek-R1~\citep{deepseekr1}; QwQ-32B~\citep{qwq} is a native reasoning model; OT-7B~\citep{openthinker} is trained with STILL~\citep{still} on Qwen2.5-7B. Data: HarmThoughts~\citep{harmthoughts} with step-level CP annotations. Split: 60/20/20.}
\label{tab:models}
\end{table}

\subsection{Probe Architecture}
\label{sec:probe-architecture}

\paragraph{Hidden-state (HS) probes.} At each step $t$, we extract the last-token hidden state $\mathbf{h}_t^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ from layer $\ell$ and train an L2-regularized logistic regression classifier (balanced class weights, $C{=}1.0$, LBFGS, max\_iter${}=2000$) on binary CP labels. Layer selection is analyzed in \cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}.

\paragraph{Text probes.} Each step's text is encoded with all-MiniLM-L6-v2~\citep{wang2020minilm} ($\mathbb{R}^{384}$), a distilled sentence encoder from the Sentence-BERT family~\citep{reimers2019sentence}. The same logistic regression is applied as a surface-level modality baseline.

\paragraph{Temporal windowing.} For window size $W$, we concatenate features from steps $[t{-}W{+}1, \ldots, t]$. We evaluate $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ and select per-model on validation. GRU sequence models are also evaluated; for fair comparison, \cref{tab:gru_multi_seeds} reports GRU results under the same 60/20/20 split used for all LR experiments (\cref{app:gru_details}).

\subsection{Layer Selection Sensitivity}
\label{sec:layer-sensitivity}

Layer selection is a researcher degree of freedom that can reverse modality comparison conclusions. A composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models: L2 (3\% depth) for QwQ-32B, L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. Shallow representations are highly compressed (inter-step cosine distances 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers), so a linear probe operates near the noise floor and threshold crossings are noise-driven (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}).

A five-metric ablation reveals that threshold-dependent metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) consistently select deep layers: L60 (94\%) for QwQ-32B, L63 (98\%) for R1-32B. Pure balanced accuracy selects middle layers (L30, L47), paralleling the AUROC pattern (\cref{sec:layer-results}). At these layers, all four models show significant HS advantage (Holm-Bonferroni corrected, \cref{sec:evaluation}); at biased layers, only the two 7--8B models survive. Because the three metrics (balanced accuracy, precision, FPR) share the same confusion matrix, the effective number of independent comparisons is four (one per model); Holm-Bonferroni step-down on four model-level tests yields the same result (all four pass, with R1-8B FPR $p{=}0.044$ at rank-4 threshold $\alpha/1{=}0.050$). A joint (layer, $W$) grid search over 192 configurations confirms that L60 is near-optimal for QwQ-32B at recommended windows ($W{=}3$--$5$; \cref{app:layer_sweep}). Full per-layer results appear in the same appendix.

\subsection{Baseline Methods}
\label{sec:baselines}

We evaluate seven alternatives to contextualize probe performance (\cref{tab:negative}): BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian} and CUSUM~\citep{page1954cusum} for change-point detection; Mahalanobis distance following TrajGuard~\citep{trajguard}; RepE mean-difference directions~\citep{zou2023representation}; two-layer MLP probes; CCS-adapted~\citep{burns2023discovering}; and cross-model transfer via PCA. A position-only baseline ($t/T$) controls for temporal artifacts. Detailed configurations in \cref{app:bocpd_config,app:transfer_details}.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:evaluation}

We report balanced accuracy, precision, pre-CP FPR (step-level and trace-level), coverage, and lead time (steps between first detection and true CP). Detection requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings, empirically selected as a middle-ground tradeoff between coverage and false positive rate (\cref{app:k_ablation}). Modality comparisons use two-sided null-centered bootstrap (10{,}000 resamples) with Holm-Bonferroni step-down correction (3 metrics $\times$ 4 models $= 12$ tests, $\alpha{=}0.05$). Within each model, the three metrics are positively correlated by construction, making the correction stricter than under independence. Control task selectivity~\citep{hewitt2019control} validates genuine representational structure (\cref{app:control}).

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We study R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, $n{=}43$ test traces), OT-7B (OpenThinker-7B; Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, $n{=}47$), QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native reasoning, $n{=}19$--$20$), and R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, $n{=}20$). All traces come from HarmThoughts with step-level CP annotations (60/20/20 split). HS probes extract last-token hidden states from a selected layer; text probes encode each step with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). Both use L2-regularized logistic regression with temporal windowing $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ selected on validation. Detection requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings. Statistical testing follows \cref{sec:evaluation}.

\subsection{Layer Selection Results}\label{sec:layer-results}

\cref{tab:layer_selection} shows that the biased composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models: L2 (3\% depth) for QwQ-32B and L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. Three of four metrics excluding crossing rate (precision, inverse FPR, and additive composite) consistently select deep layers: L60 (94\%) and L63 (98\%). Pure balanced accuracy, a threshold-free metric, selects middle layers (L30 and L47), consistent with the AUROC pattern discussed in \cref{sec:discussion}. The mechanism is that shallow layers produce highly compressed representations with small inter-step margins: step-to-step cosine distances are 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers across all four models (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}). A linear probe in this compressed space operates near the noise floor, generating frequent threshold crossings that inflate crossing rate independently of discriminative power (\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Biased}$^a$ & \textbf{Corrected}$^b$ & $p$ \textbf{(corr.)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 (44\%) & L14 (44\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
OT-7B & L0 (0\%) & L16 (57\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
QwQ-32B & L2 (3\%) & L60 (94\%) & ${<}0.005$ \\
R1-32B & L10 (16\%) & L63 (98\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Layer selection sensitivity. $^a$\,bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate. $^b$\,Consensus of threshold-dependent metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite). Rightmost: HS vs.\ text precision $p$-value at corrected layer (e.g., QwQ-32B biased-layer: bal\_acc $p{=}0.35$, precision $p{=}0.83$, both non-significant).}
\label{tab:layer_selection}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_layer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Per-layer metric heatmap (2$\times$2). Each column: one of five metrics (min-max normalized). Red dashed: biased layer (bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate); green dashed: corrected layer (consensus of threshold-dependent metrics). For R1-8B, both coincide at L14. At 32B, biased selections cluster near input layers while threshold-dependent metrics peak at deep layers.}
\label{fig:layer_heatmap}
\end{figure}

The downstream impact is severe: at biased layers, only the two 7--8B models show significant HS advantage; both 32B models fail. At corrected layers, all four models show significant HS advantage (Holm-Bonferroni corrected, \cref{sec:evaluation}). Had we reported only biased-layer results, we would have concluded a scale-dependent HS advantage that is entirely a methodological artifact.

\subsection{HS vs.\ Text Comparison}\label{sec:hs-text}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llclrrlrc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Enc.} & $W$ & $\Delta$\textbf{BA} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec} & \textbf{95\% CI} & $\Delta$\textbf{FPR} & \textbf{HB} \\
\midrule
